sorted desc stable: [new_https, https_default, new_http, http_default].\n\nSo `session.adapters[0]` is the newly mounted https adapter. And `get_adapter(\"https://...\")` returns the first adapter whose prefix matches: new_https. Good.\n\nSo asserting `session.adapters[0] is adapter`... but we don't have a reference to the adapter since it's created inside the function. We could assert `session.adapters[0].max_retries is retry` for the Retry instance case. That's a strong assertion.\n\nActually `get_adapter` is cleaner. Let me use `session.get_adapter(\"https://example.com\")` and `session.get_adapter(\"http://example.com\")`.\n\nFor the Retry instance case: `adapter.max_retries is retry` \u2014 strong identity check.\n\nNow, about the hook test: `session.hooks[\"response\"][0]` is a lambda taking `(r, *args, **kwargs)`. Calling `hook(response)` calls `response.raise_for_status()`. Using a real `requests.models.Response` with status_code=500: `raise_for_status` raises HTTPError. Good.\n\nAlso test that a 200 response doesn't raise: `hook(ok_response)` returns None (lambda returns r.raise_for_status() which returns None for 200). Fine.\n\nNow, about cleanup: sessions opened \u2014 `Session.close()` closes adapters (connection pools). Using a fixture to close them is good. Alternatively, just call `session.close()` at the end of each test. A fixture is cleaner.\n\nAlso, no monkeypatching is strictly needed since the function doesn't touch global state. But the instructions say \"Be sure to include assertions... make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution; use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate.\" Closing sessions is the main cleanup. I could also use `mocker` from pytest-mock, but it's not necessary. I'll use a simple fixture for closing sessions.\n\nLet me also double check the import path: `from semantic_release.helpers import build_requests_session`. The snippet says \"extracted from semantic_release/helpers.py\". Good.\n\nLet me now call get_info to verify the function exists and check its signature.\n\nI'll call get_info(\"build_requests_session\") first.\n\nThen write the final test.\n\nOne more consideration: the `test_retry_invalid_type` test \u2014 the ValueError is raised before any adapter mounting, but the session was already created (line 26). The session is not returned, so it leaks. Not a big deal, but we can't close it easily. It's fine \u2014 garbage collected.\n\nAlso, should I test `retry=0`? `if retry:` \u2192 False \u2192 no mounting. That covers line 29's False branch with an int. The `retry=False` test already covers the False branch. Adding `retry=0` is optional; I'll include it for branch completeness of the truthiness check (same branch though). Actually branch coverage: `if retry:` has two branches \u2014 truthy and falsy. `retry=False` covers falsy. Fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether `session.hooks` default: `default_hooks()` returns `{\"response\": []}`. So for raise_for_status=False, `session.hooks == {\"response\": []}`. I'll assert `session.hooks.get(\"response\") == []`.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double-check requests' default_hooks:\n\n```python\ndef default_hooks():\n    return {event: [] for event in HOOKS}\n```\n\nand `HOOKS = [\"response\"]`. So yes, `{\"response\": []}`.\n\nNow the final test script. Let me also add a test verifying the hook passes through extra args (the `*args, **kwargs` in the lambda) \u2014 calling `hook(response, \"arg\", key=\"value\")` should work without TypeError. That covers the lambda's parameter handling (though lambda lines are within line 28's coverage).\n\nLet me write it:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Tests for semantic_release.helpers.build_requests_session (lines 26-39).\"\"\"\n\nimport pytest\nimport requests\nfrom requests import Session\nfrom requests.adapters import HTTPAdapter\nfrom requests.models import Response\nfrom requests.packages.urllib3.util.retry import Retry\n\nfrom semantic_release.helpers import build_requests_session\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef keep_sessions():\n    \"\"\"Collect sessions created in a test and close them afterwards.\"\"\"\n    created = []\n\n    def register(session):\n        created.append(session)\n        return session\n\n    yield register\n\n    for session in created:\n        session.close()\n\n\ndef test_build_requests_session_defaults(keep_sessions):\n    \"\"\"Default call: raise_for_status hook installed, default Retry mounted.\"\"\"\n    session = keep_sessions(build_requests_session())\n\n    assert isinstance(session, Session)\n    # raise_for_status hook installed by default\n    hooks = session.hooks.get(\"response\", [])\n    assert len(hooks) == 1\n\n    # a default Retry configuration is mounted for both schemes\n    for url in (\"http://example.com\", \"https://example.com\"):\n        adapter = session.get_adapter(url)\n        assert isinstance(adapter, HTTPAdapter)\n        assert isinstance(adapter.max_retries, Retry)\n        assert adapter.max_retries.total == Retry().total\n\n\ndef test_build_requests_session_hook_raises_on_error_status(keep_sessions):\n    \"\"\"The installed hook calls raise_for_status on the response.\"\"\"\n    session = keep_sessions(build_requests_session())\n    hook = session.hooks[\"response\"][0]\n\n    bad = Response()\n    bad.status_code = 500\n    with pytest.raises(requests.HTTPError):\n        hook(bad, timeout=5, stream=True)  # extra args/kwargs must be accepted\n\n    ok = Response()\n    ok.status_code = 200\n    assert hook(ok) is None\n\n\ndef test_build_requests_session_without_raise_for_status(keep_sessions):\n    \"\"\"raise_for_status=False: no hook installed, retry still configured.\"\"\"\n    session = keep_sessions(build_requests_session(raise_for_status=False))\n\n    assert isinstance(session, Session)\n    assert session.hooks.get(\"response\", []) == []\n\n    adapter = session.get_adapter(\"https://example.com\")\n    assert isinstance(adapter, HTTPAdapter)\n    as